Traps this pole exists to catch:

  · The fragment is BYTE-FOR-BYTE identical to the violation pole from
    "## Where we are" onward — same ledger, same "Read first", same in-flight
    paragraph, same sentinels. A judge keying on overall polish, section
    completeness, or register will score both poles the same way. The only
    separating signal is the directives section's contract.
  · The `Traps` section here is LONGER than the violation pole's, because two
    items were correctly demoted into it. A judge treating a long Traps section
    as clutter, or as evidence that the operator could not decide what mattered,
    has inverted the contract — demotion into Traps IS the documented remedy.
  · Directive 3 is phrased as a prohibition rather than an action. The documented
    property is "imperative", which a prohibition satisfies; a judge requiring a
    positive verb would flag a compliant row.
  · Three directives, not five. The cap is a ceiling, not a target; a judge
    treating the unused slots as an omission has misread it.
